  "limits": [
      "A phrase is an extra search key for that same catalogue row. It is not a claim that two foods have the same nutrients.",
      "Queries match the whole normalized phrase. Tokens are not OR-ed, so 'chicken' does not pull in every chicken-breast phrase.",
      "Elk is not aliased to moose: in US English elk is a different animal from European elg/moose.",
      "Shrimp and king prawn stay separate. Saithe is coley, not Alaska pollock. Sugar snap is not a snow pea. Swede is not kohlrabi.",
      "Plant drinks are not aliased to milk. Chips, crisps, and fries are not aliased to each other.",
      "Paprika powder is not given the vegetable phrases, and raw coriander leaf is the only row aliased to cilantro.",
      "Official names that are already a single searchable phrase are left alone. Case and spacing of an existing name are not stored again.",
      "A word names the same food in Norwegian, Swedish and Finnish. A table's row gets the other two languages' words, so 'kyckling' finds Norwegian chicken and 'kylling' finds Finnish broileri.",
      "Words match the first word of a table's own name: the word itself, or with a star a compound that starts with it ('kycklingbröst') or ends with it ('grillimakkara'). A compound that names a dish made with the food ('porokeitto', 'kinkkupiirakka') is not the food; see dishEnds.",
      "Vegetables, fruit and berries match the whole first word only: 'gulrotkake' is a cake, not a carrot.",
      "Cream and milk substitutes keep their own names, as plant drinks do. Baltic herring (strömming, silakka) is not aliased to Atlantic herring (sild).",
      "A word is added alone, not joined to the rest of the name, so a foreign phrase like 'kyckling bröstfilé' still finds that country's own row."
  ],
